I see the bug clearly now. In `read_memoryimmediate`:

1. Line 472: `data[1] = get_varuint32(&code[len], code_len - len, &tmp);`
2. Line 473: `if (len == -1) {` — **BUG**: should check `tmp == -1`, not `len == -1`

Since `len` was just set to `tmp` (a valid positive value), `len == -1` is always false. So if `get_varuint32` returns -1 in `tmp` (indicating an error), the code doesn't detect it and continues.

But more importantly, when `get_varuint32` returns -1 in `tmp`, the function also returns 0 in `data[1]`. Then at line 481-483, it writes to `MI->flat_insn->detail->wasm.operands[1].size = tmp` where `tmp = -1` (which is `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` as a `size_t`). This sets `size` to a huge value, but that's just a field in the struct, not a memory safety issue.

Wait, let me look at the `get_varuint32` function again:

```c
static uint32_t get_varuint32(const uint8_t *code, size_t code_len, size_t *leng)
{
    uint32_t data = 0;
    int i;
    for(i = 0;; i++) {
        if (code_len < i + 1) {
            *leng = -1;
            return 0;
        }
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

When `code_len < i + 1`, it sets `*leng = -1` and returns 0. So `data[1]` would be 0, and `tmp` would be `-1` (i.e., `SIZE_MAX`).

Then at line 481: `MI->flat_insn->detail->wasm.operands[1].size = tmp;` — this sets `size` to `SIZE_MAX` (truncated to `uint8_t`, so 255).

And at line 483: `MI->flat_insn->detail->wasm.operands[1].varuint32 = data[1];` — this sets `varuint32` to 0.

Then at line 486: `len += tmp;` — this adds `SIZE_MAX` to `len`, which could wrap around on 32-bit but on 64-bit it just becomes a huge number.

Then at line 487: `MI->wasm_data.size = len;` — this truncates to `uint16_t` (since `size` is `uint16_t` in the struct).

